  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-109012",
    "knowledge_point_id": "45b380ed-1974-5876-8c88-a45773741b48",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Particular Moment",
    "problem": "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in miles) at time $t$ hours is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{3}{t},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=3\\,\\text{h}.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=3,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(3)=-\\frac{3}{{3}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-\\frac{1}{3}|=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{miles/h}.$",
